#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1666c48c-616a-5584-b4b9-47f389d5edd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545240} {:answer/id #uuid "269226e3-2066-5e11-9e53-68cf25388793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545283} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab4711a-55c0-5e5e-90df-e23264046ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545478} {:answer/id #uuid "daf3c561-12b4-504e-a0d4-870b426bed38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545606} {:answer/id #uuid "dec293c4-9e4a-569e-b668-6abc7a7c73a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dec293c4-9e4a-569e-b668-6abc7a7c73a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545610} :answer-field/id #uuid "4635540b-1236-5d34-8d5b-e01f27623485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94febb74-e81c-5137-a80c-9d7fa83538e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141229" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 5$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d562ea0c-94f3-5fe6-ba82-c651a51d0bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Given a Description of the Column or Null Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2237675a-cbb8-55f4-ac1e-48c201dc4645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545270} {:answer/id #uuid "b366e033-8b4f-533d-86e0-d5b107bc8270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545525} {:answer/id #uuid "d71b0cc6-29c4-5b34-b310-64ab60f5b724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545592} {:answer/id #uuid "d90fb75f-16a5-5779-817f-22c451b4191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545597} {:answer/id #uuid "fe29b5e6-ad57-590e-8812-0f23650c5a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d71b0cc6-29c4-5b34-b310-64ab60f5b724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a810a855-c6d2-5045-a468-45c2987823ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e7c8be1-d271-506d-bfc9-fbf1881a88d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-142489" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17adadd-02fe-5b13-a556-c4b68557dec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183140} {:answer/id #uuid "f44f8717-6bc7-5c29-a85d-01286768fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183141} {:answer/id #uuid "3493b4eb-bc91-59c3-abe3-1362d436c2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183142} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d43af-17a2-5f2e-916b-b2453eace3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183143} {:answer/id #uuid "62186aa1-6d7f-5a56-8e00-28c657c126cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f44f8717-6bc7-5c29-a85d-01286768fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183141} :answer-field/id #uuid "405282dd-7ce6-56bf-8492-d4b7cbb14f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d185801-460b-5883-a896-8f1107477aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34930" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}?\n$$\n\nI. The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the generating vectors in \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nare linearly independent, we conclude that the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4.$ Now, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3ba979-73eb-55d9-98be-53eb63f15310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454018} {:answer/id #uuid "42f7e70e-a33c-5389-83b8-4b66c4b40413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f7e70e-a33c-5389-83b8-4b66c4b40413" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454019} :answer-field/id #uuid "51beb1c0-c434-5ffe-be4f-d1af3a966f94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b27c5d69-4610-55e4-befd-bab1f9f21b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454020} {:answer/id #uuid "7f38039b-9493-5449-afe0-51512728ff71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f38039b-9493-5449-afe0-51512728ff71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454021} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd2f4dc-0220-560a-b969-e0fb63f01a28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454760} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22168706-fdd0-5ec9-8357-a478a254b0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454022} {:answer/id #uuid "b3eaef39-5ed2-5d0e-8448-e5d92a445686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22168706-fdd0-5ec9-8357-a478a254b0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454022} :answer-field/id #uuid "447c453c-9c02-53db-ad8b-23d0593303f4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c7ff497-8845-5d34-a5a1-e0b10d7a1e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-79367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e145cb2-806a-579d-8299-8a91a6feaad1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem and the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1b4ae3-0a40-5756-bb35-332f91366150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545226} {:answer/id #uuid "341ea572-fea0-5ab5-8e97-94b9de3afc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545303} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc8c0b7-7f8f-5ba1-a69b-9e3d9da97a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545318} {:answer/id #uuid "73232096-0842-59cd-811b-a3783597953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545416} {:answer/id #uuid "855804bf-27d3-5391-ba94-a4c1ff073b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f1b4ae3-0a40-5756-bb35-332f91366150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545226} :answer-field/id #uuid "c143e0e6-92b8-5ab3-a31a-0f6f3f8892c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c10d4c77-93c5-52a9-900d-275fa9299d3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79410" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $A$ has $2$ pivot colums" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the non-zero vector\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nspans $\\text{Null}(A),$ we conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=1.$ So, by the rank-nullity theorem, with $n=4$,  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=3,$ we must have  that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The fact that $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=3$ implies that the number of pivot columns in $A$ is also equal to $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
